+# Implementation Plan: Task-Handoff MCP Server Accepts Standard Client Metadata in Tool Calls
+
+**Branch**: `main` (single source-of-truth checkout; setup JSON reports `BRANCH: main`, and no `before_specify` branch hook is registered) | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Spec**: [specs/001-bench/spec.md](spec.md)
+
+**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-bench/spec.md`
+
+## Summary
+
+The task-handoff MCP stdio adapter (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, launched as
+`--mcp-stdio`) rejects every `tools/call` whose `params` object carries a field other than `name`
+and `arguments`. Native MCP clients attach the standard `_meta` metadata object (e.g.
+`{"progressToken": ...}`) to requests, so every such call fails with a JSON-RPC `-32602` invalid
+request before dispatch. The fix relaxes exactly one rule in the `tools/call` branch of
+`_mcp_stdio()`: admit an object-valued `_meta` field and treat it as opaque (never read it again).
+All neighboring strictness — other unknown params fields, non-object `_meta`, identity-override in
+tool arguments, and the oversized-frame refusal — keeps its exact current behavior. The fixed file
+must stay byte-for-byte identical with its distributed twin in the Codex companion plugin, and the
+adapter's own unittest file gains an end-to-end stdio case proving a `_meta` call reaches dispatch.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Python 3.11 (3.11.9 observed on this machine). The module uses
+`from __future__ import annotations`, so its newer syntax is annotation-only and imposes no lower
+bound beyond what the rest of the plugin already requires; target what the host installs — the
+tests run under whatever `sys.executable` the machine provides.
+
+**Primary Dependencies**: Python standard library only (`json`, `os`, `sys`, `argparse`,
+`hashlib`, `datetime`, `urllib.parse`) plus the local sibling module `session_hook`. **No new
+dependency may be added**; if one ever were, it must be named in the PR body per the delivery rules.
+
+**Storage**: N/A for this change. The adapter reads execution state over HTTP from the GraphHelm
+Runtime (`session_hook.request`) using a token from `GRAPHHELM_TOKEN_FILE`; nothing in this fix
+touches persistence. Tests use a local loopback `ThreadingHTTPServer` fake of the Runtime.
+
+**Testing**: Standard-library `unittest` in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py`.
+End-to-end coverage drives the real process via `subprocess.run([sys.executable, SCRIPT,
+"--mcp-stdio"], input=...)` with newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdin and parses the stdout
+responses. The suite binds 127.0.0.1:0 only — no internet, no credentials, no Docker.
+
+**Target Platform**: Windows PowerShell (this machine, verified) and any platform Python 3.11
+runs on; the plugin must stay cross-platform. No OS-specific constructs are introduced.
+
+**Project Type**: CLI / stdio MCP server shipped as a plugin hook script inside a larger monorepo
+(the Rust workspace crates live outside `plugins/` and are untouched by this feature).
+
+**Performance Goals**: N/A. Single-process line-oriented protocol already bounded by
+`MAX_MCP_MESSAGE = 32 * 1024`; the spec sets no latency or throughput SLO. The cost of the change
+is one set-difference and one `isinstance` check per `tools/call`.
+
+**Constraints**:
+- Byte-for-byte identity between `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` (FR-007) after the change.
+- All rejections must occur before dispatch with **no runtime side effects** (FR-003…FR-006):
+  no event/evidence/briefing HTTP calls may be made for an invalid call.
+- `_meta` is opaque: admitted only if it is a JSON object, never interpreted, never echoed (FR-001, FR-002).
+- No new dependencies, no new imports, no new modules, functions, or public surface.
+- Previously passing tests keep their observable expectations unchanged (FR-009); new coverage is
+  additive (new test method(s), not edits to existing assertions).
+- All documentation in this repository is written in English.
+
+**Scale/Scope**: One validation rule in `_mcp_stdio()` (today the two-line check around line 495
+of the adapter), mirrored into the twin copy, plus new test case(s) in the one existing test file.
+Three files change in total: the adapter, its twin, and the test file.
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+*GATE: Must pass before Phase 0 research. Re-check after Phase 1 design.*
+
+This checkout has no `.specify/memory/constitution.md`; per the run setup, **`AGENTS.md` at the
+repository root is used as the governance reference** for this gate (substitution recorded here as
+instructed). Gates extracted from AGENTS.md that bind this feature:
+
+| # | Gate (AGENTS.md source) | Status | Evidence / how satisfied |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| G1 | Journey-Proven Development: every promised behavior has a smallest adequate observer at the correct boundary; no ceremonial scaffold | PASS | The promise is "a `_meta` call reaches dispatch over stdio"; the observer is a subprocess stdio test (same boundary the user hits) asserting a `result` reply plus a hit on the fake Runtime, mirroring existing MCP tests. No proxy acceptance: dispatch is observed, not inferred from exit code alone. |
+| G2 | Keel proportionality: bounded code change → direct route; card names paths, promise, proving command; add only surface the promise needs | PASS | Diff touches the existing `tools/call` branch body in one function plus additive tests. No new type, helper, module, or interface (see research R2 for the rejected helper alternative). The implementing PR carries the three-line card (paths, promise, command from quickstart.md). |
+| G3 | Security rules: repository/protocol input is untrusted; bound before expensive work; no expression/shell/network execution during validation; secrets stay out of outputs | PASS | `_meta` is admitted only after the whole frame has passed the existing 32 KiB bound and `json.loads`; the check is an `isinstance(dict)` on already-parsed data — no network, shell, or model call. Its contents are discarded immediately and never serialized into responses, so they cannot leak or influence results. |
+| G4 | Dependencies: no new dependency without being named in the PR body | PASS | Standard library and the local `session_hook` module only; zero new imports. |
+| G5 | Documentation language: everything under `docs/`, specs, ADRs, etc. is English | PASS | All artifacts generated here (plan, research, data-model, contracts, quickstart) are written in English. |
+| G6 | Repository boundaries: business rules in the owning module; no cross-boundary imports | PASS | Change stays entirely inside `plugins/**/hooks/`; no Rust workspace crate, schema, or CLI contract is touched. The MCP wire shape extended is documented in `contracts/mcp-stdio-tools-call.md`. |
+| G7 | Test discipline: fixed, deterministic assertions; no wall-clock/randomness/filesystem-order dependence; tests offline | PASS | New tests reuse the deterministic subprocess + loopback fake pattern; assertions are on JSON-RPC codes, result flags, and request counters — not timestamps or ordering. |
+
+Post-design re-check (after Phase 1): still **PASS** for all gates. The design adds no surface
+beyond what G2 assumes (two lines of validation in-place + additive tests), so no complexity is
+justified below.
+
