+# Quickstart: Validate "Accept Standard Client Metadata in MCP Tool Calls"
+
+**Feature**: specs/001-bench/spec.md · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+Runnable validation scenarios proving the feature end-to-end from this checkout. No internet,
+Docker, credentials, or provider accounts needed — the suite uses a loopback fake of the
+GraphHelm Runtime. Commands are written for Windows PowerShell (the machine this checkout lives
+on); bash equivalents are noted where they differ.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- Python 3.11.x on PATH (3.11.9 observed on this machine).
+- This checkout, unmodified state or with the feature implemented.
+- Working directory = repository root.
+
+## Baseline (recorded before the change)
+
+- `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py`: **9 tests, all passing** (~1.2 s).
+- The two distributed `task_handoff.py` copies are **byte-identical** (27 443 bytes each).
+
+## Step 1 — Adapter test suite (behavioral proof; SC-001, SC-002, SC-003, SC-005)
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_task_handoff -v
+```
+
+<sub>bash: `python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_task_handoff -v`</sub>
+
+**Expected after implementation**
+
+- Every one of the 9 pre-existing tests passes **unchanged** (FR-009; includes the existing
+  identity-override + oversized-frame stdio tests, which prove SC-003).
+- At least one **new** test exists whose name/purpose covers the standard-metadata call and it
+  passes: a real subprocess stdio session sends `tools/call` with an object-valued `_meta`, and the
+  assertions show dispatch — a `result` reply (not `-32602`), `"isError": false`, and a hit on the
+  fake Runtime — plus negative legs still rejected (`_meta` as non-object; another unknown params
+  field), each with `-32602` and zero Runtime requests (SC-001, SC-002, FR-008).
+- Exit code 0. Any failure is a defect: read the failing test name against the
+  [contract](contracts/mcp-stdio-tools-call.md) rejection table.
+
+## Step 2 — Distribution byte identity (SC-004, FR-007)
+
+```powershell
+Get-FileHash plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py, plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py -Algorithm SHA256 | Format-Table Hash, Path -AutoSize
+```
+
+<sub>bash:
+`sha256sum plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`</sub>
+
+**Expected**: both paths report the **same SHA-256**. A mismatch means the Codex companion copy was
+not synced from the main plugin file — restore it verbatim and re-check before anything else.
+
+## Step 3 — Optional manual stdio probe (no Runtime available)
+
+Proves the accept/reject discriminator directly on the wire, without a live GraphHelm Runtime.
+The probe points the runtime URL at a closed local port, so a *dispatched* call still fails at the
+network level — the observable point is the **envelope class**, not the tool outcome:
+
+```powershell
+$env:GRAPHHELM_EXECUTION_ID = "run-probe"
+$env:GRAPHHELM_TOKEN_FILE = "$PWD\README.md"          # any readable file; contents are never echoed
+$env:GRAPHHELM_RUNTIME_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:9"     # closed port -> instant connection refusal
+$env:GRAPHHELM_MCP_HOST = "codex"
+$env:GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID = "probe-session"
+
+$accepted = '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"status","arguments":{},"_meta":{"progressToken":"pt-1"}}}'
+$badMeta  = '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"status","arguments":{},"_meta":"pt-1"}}'
+
+# Two frames, one per line on stdin:
+"$accepted`n$badMeta`n" | python plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py --mcp-stdio
+```
+
+(Unset the five environment variables afterwards if they were not yours.)
+
+**Expected output**
+
+Frame outcomes are identical pre- and post-fix *except* for frame 1:
+
+| Frame | Pre-fix | After implementation |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 — `_meta` object | `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"invalid request"}}` | `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"handoff tool failed; result is unobserved"}],"isError":true}}` |
+| 2 — `_meta` string | `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"invalid request"}}` | unchanged |
+| 3 — PowerShell-only artifact | `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":null,"error":{"code":-32602,...}}` | unchanged |
+
+The tool-level failure text in frame 1's post-fix result is *expected* (unreachable runtime) and
+is exactly what distinguishes "dispatched" from "rejected": a rejected call yields an `error`
+envelope, a dispatched call a `result` envelope. Frame 3 appears only because PowerShell appends a
+trailing newline to piped input (an empty frame); it is harmless and absent when driving stdin from
+bash or any exact-byte writer. Exit code is `0` in both cases (clean EOF).
+
+## Outcome mapping
+
+| Success criterion | Observed by |
+|---|---|
+| SC-001 (meta calls reach dispatch) | Step 1 new test, positive leg; Step 3 frame 1 |
+| SC-002 (other unknown fields still rejected) | Step 1 negative leg(s); pre-existing rejection tests |
+| SC-003 (identity-override / oversized unchanged) | Step 1 pre-existing `test_mcp_rejects_identity_override_and_oversized_request_before_runtime_io` and oversized-frame tests |
+| SC-004 (byte-identical copies) | Step 2 |
+| SC-005 (suite green + new passing meta case) | Step 1 summary line (`Ran N tests ... OK`) |
+
+## Out of scope for validation here
+
+Version-bump behavior (deferred per research R4), other MCP methods (frozen by contract, covered by
+pre-existing tests), and the Rust workspace gate — this feature changes no crate, schema, or CLI
+binary, so no `cargo` check is part of its minimal observation set.
