+# Feature Specification: Task-Handoff MCP Server Accepts Standard Client Metadata in Tool Calls
+
+**Feature Branch**: none created — no `before_specify` branch hook is registered in this checkout (`.specify/extensions.yml` absent); the work stays on the current branch of this single source-of-truth checkout.
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "The task-handoff MCP stdio adapter (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, `--mcp-stdio`) refuses any `tools/call` whose `params` has a field other than `name` and `arguments`. Native MCP clients send the standard `_meta` object (for example `{\"progressToken\": ...}`), so every tool call from them fails before dispatch. Accept `_meta` when it is an object; every other unknown params field, the identity-override refusal and the oversized-frame refusal must keep working. The Codex companion (`plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`) ships the same file and must stay byte-identical. Cover the `_meta` call in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py`."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Native MCP clients can actually use the task-handoff tools (Priority: P1)
+
+Agent runtimes that speak the Model Context Protocol (MCP) over a standard-input connection attach a metadata object (`_meta`, e.g. carrying a `progressToken`) to tool calls as part of their standard client behavior. Today the task-handoff server treats that metadata as an unrecognized parameter and rejects the call as invalid before the requested tool ever runs. As a result, every task-handoff tool — offering work, receiving it, or checking its status — is unusable from any such native client, defeating the point of exposing these tools over MCP at all. After this change, the server accepts the standard metadata object and dispatches the call exactly as it would an otherwise identical call that carries no metadata.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the core defect. Until it is fixed, the primary use case — a hosted agent session handing tasks to or receiving them from a native MCP client through the task-handoff tools — fails on every call. Everything else in this feature exists to protect what this fix must not break.
+
+**Independent Test**: Start the task-handoff server on its standard-input endpoint with a valid host and session identity, then send one well-formed tool call whose parameters carry the tool name, tool arguments, and a metadata object. The expected outcome is a normal tool result for the named tool — not an invalid-request error — proving the call reached dispatch. This test exercises the complete user value of the fix by itself.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the task-handoff server running on its standard-input endpoint with a valid host and session identity, **When** a tool-call request arrives whose parameters contain the tool name, the tool arguments, and a `_meta` object (for example one carrying a `progressToken`), **Then** the request is dispatched to the named tool and returns the same kind of normal tool result an equivalent request without `_meta` would have returned.
+2. **Given** the same running server, **When** a tool-call request arrives whose `_meta` is an empty object, **Then** the request is likewise dispatched normally; the presence or content of the metadata does not change which tool runs, how it runs, or what the tool result says.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The server stays strict about everything the fix is not about (Priority: P2)
+
+Accepting the standard metadata object is a deliberate relaxation of one rule only. The server's other strictness — rejecting tool calls whose parameters carry any *other* unrecognized field, rejecting calls that try to override the server's own host/session identity from inside the tool arguments, and rejecting physical frames larger than the message limit — exists so that untrusted input cannot change how the server identifies itself or read garbage past a well-formed message. Those protections must keep working exactly as they do today, including being applied before any runtime effect occurs.
+
+**Why this priority**: These behaviors are the security boundary of the adapter. A fix that loosens one rule while silently loosening its neighbors would trade a compatibility bug for a correctness or trust bug, which is worse. It ranks below Story 1 because it protects the fix rather than delivering new capability.
+
+**Independent Test**: Against the fixed server, send tool calls each carrying one different violation — a non-standard extra parameter field, a `_meta` value that is not an object, an identity-override inside the tool arguments, and an overlong frame — and confirm each is still rejected with the same invalid-request behavior as before, without any runtime side effect.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the fixed server, **When** a tool-call request arrives whose parameters contain a field other than the tool name, the tool arguments, and `_meta`, **Then** the request is rejected as invalid before dispatch and produces no runtime side effects.
+2. **Given** the fixed server, **When** a tool-call request arrives whose `_meta` field is present but not an object (a string, number, list, or null), **Then** the request is rejected as invalid before dispatch and produces no runtime side effects.
+3. **Given** the fixed server, **When** a tool-call request carries values inside the tool arguments that attempt to set the host or session identity, **Then** the request is refused exactly as it is refused today.
+4. **Given** the fixed server, **When** an incoming frame exceeds the established message-size limit, **Then** the server answers invalid request and ends the connection without interpreting the frame's remaining bytes as another request — exactly as today.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - Every distributed copy gets the same fix, proven by the shipped tests (Priority: P3)
+
+The adapter ships in two places: the main GraphHelm plugin and the Codex companion plugin. Distributors and downstream checks rely on those two files being byte-for-byte identical, so a fix that lands in only one copy silently forks the behavior of the two plugins. In addition, the project's delivery discipline requires that a new accepted input shape be demonstrated by the adapter's own automated tests rather than asserted, so the standard-metadata case must be covered where the adapter's tests already live.
+
+**Why this priority**: Without byte identity, the fix exists only half; without the shipped test, the fix has no durable proof in this repository. Both are cheap and mechanical once the fix itself works, so they trail the behavioral stories but must land together with the change.
+
+**Independent Test**: Compare the two distributed adapter files byte-for-byte after the change and expect an exact match; run the adapter's automated tests and confirm the new standard-metadata case passes alongside every previously passing case.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the fix applied to the main plugin copy of the adapter, **When** the two distributed copies are compared byte-for-byte, **Then** they are exactly identical.
+2. **Given** the completed change, **When** the adapter's automated tests are run, **Then** at least one test drives a standard-metadata tool call end-to-end through the standard-input path and asserts it is dispatched (not rejected), and every previously passing test still passes unchanged.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- `_meta` arrives together with another unknown parameter field: the request is still rejected — accepting `_meta` does not make other unrecognized fields acceptable.
+- `_meta` arrives with a null or primitive value instead of an object: rejected as invalid, treated the same as any malformed parameter.
+- `_meta` arrives but the tool name or arguments are themselves missing or malformed: existing handling of `name`/`arguments` applies unchanged; accepting `_meta` adds no exception for them.
+- A very large `_meta` payload: bounded only by the existing whole-frame size limit; an oversized frame is still rejected before dispatch regardless of where the bulk sits.
+- A metadata field sent on methods other than `tools/call` (e.g. during initialization): out of scope; the fix changes validation for tool calls only, and every other method behaves exactly as today.
+- The same frame carrying both a valid `_meta` object and an identity-override inside the arguments: the identity-override refusal still wins.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The server MUST accept a `_meta` parameter on tool calls when its value is an object, and MUST dispatch such a call identically to the same call without `_meta` (same tool selected, same effective arguments, same result semantics).
+- **FR-002**: The server MUST treat the contents of `_meta` as opaque metadata: it MUST NOT use `_meta` to select a tool, alter identities, influence the tool result, or otherwise change behavior beyond admitting the request.
+- **FR-003**: The server MUST continue to reject, before dispatch and with no runtime side effects, any tool call whose parameters contain a field other than the tool name, the tool arguments, and `_meta`.
+- **FR-004**: The server MUST reject, before dispatch, a tool call whose `_meta` value is not an object.
+- **FR-005**: The identity-override refusal MUST remain in force unchanged: tool arguments that attempt to set the server's host or session identity are refused exactly as before.
+- **FR-006**: The oversized-frame refusal MUST remain unchanged: a frame beyond the message-size limit yields an invalid-request response and terminates the connection without its remaining bytes being interpreted as further requests.
+- **FR-007**: The two distributed copies of the adapter — `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` and `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` — MUST remain byte-for-byte identical after the change.
+- **FR-008**: The adapter's automated tests in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py` MUST include at least one case that sends a `tools/call` over the standard-input path whose parameters include an object-valued `_meta`, and that asserts the call is dispatched rather than rejected as invalid.
+- **FR-009**: All previously passing automated tests for the adapter MUST continue to pass without modification to their observable expectations (except where a test's very purpose is the relaxed rule, which no existing test has).
+
+### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*
+
+- **Tool-call request frame**: One newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 message on the server's standard-input endpoint; for a tool call it carries a method, a request id, and a parameters object holding the tool name, the tool arguments, and — after this change — optionally an object-valued `_meta` field.
+- **Server session identity**: The host and session pair held by the server process itself (supplied at startup, never by callers); the boundary the identity-override refusal protects.
+- **Distributed adapter copies**: The two shipped file copies described in FR-007, which form a distribution contract of byte-for-byte equality independent of the behavioral fix.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 100% of well-formed tool calls from native clients that carry an object-valued `_meta` reach dispatch and return a normal tool result; before the change, 0% did (every such call was rejected before dispatch).
+- **SC-002**: Zero tool calls are dispatched when their parameters contain any field other than the tool name, tool arguments, and `_meta`; each such call receives the same invalid-request response it received before the change.
+- **SC-003**: Zero observable behavior change in the identity-override refusal or the oversized-frame refusal; both refuse exactly the requests they refused before, at the same stage (before dispatch / before parsing, respectively).
+- **SC-004**: A byte comparison of the two distributed adapter copies reports a 100% match after the change.
+- **SC-005**: The adapter's automated suite reports zero regressions and gains at least one passing case that proves the accepted `_meta` call end-to-end over the standard-input path.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **Governance substitution**: This checkout has no `.specify/memory/constitution.md`; per the run setup, `AGENTS.md` at the repository root serves as the governance reference, and this substitution is recorded here as instructed.
+- **Scope boundary**: Only parameter validation for tool calls on the server's standard-input endpoint changes. Request handling for all other methods (initialization, ping, tool listing) and every CLI phase of the adapter behave exactly as before.
+- **Opaque metadata**: `_meta` is admitted but not interpreted. Its keys (including `progressToken`) are not consumed, validated beyond "it is an object", or echoed back in responses; within the frame-size limit, its content has no observable effect.
+- **Existing validations untouched**: Rejection of missing or malformed tool names and arguments keeps today's exact behavior; the fix introduces no new exceptions for them.
+- **Pre-state verified**: At specification time the two distributed adapter copies were measured byte-identical (matching SHA-256), and the adapter's automated tests exist only under the main plugin path named in FR-008; the Codex companion ships no separate test file, so "byte-identical copies" fully covers test consistency between the two plugins.
+- **Unattended execution**: No clarification round was performed. Every open question in the description had a reasonable default (documented above), so the specification contains no open clarification markers of the kind this template reserves for unresolved decisions.
+- **Branching**: No `before_specify` hook is registered, so no feature branch was created or switched; this single checkout is the source of truth and all changes remain on disk here.
