+# Tasks: Task-Handoff MCP Server Accepts Standard Client Metadata in Tool Calls
+
+**Input**: Design documents from `/specs/001-bench/`
+
+**Prerequisites**: plan.md (required), spec.md (required for user stories), research.md, data-model.md, contracts/mcp-stdio-tools-call.md, quickstart.md — all present.
+
+**Governance substitution (recorded)**: This checkout has no `.specify/memory/constitution.md`; per the run setup, **`AGENTS.md` at the repository root is the governance reference** for this command (same substitution plan.md and spec.md already record).
+
+**Extension hooks**: `.specify/extensions.yml` does not exist, so no `before_tasks`/`after_tasks` hook is registered and all hooks are skipped (per setup instructions).
+
+**Tests**: REQUESTED. The feature specification explicitly requires shipped test coverage — FR-008 ("the adapter's automated tests … MUST include at least one case that sends a `tools/call` … with an object-valued `_meta`"), FR-009 (previously passing tests keep their expectations), SC-005 (suite gains a passing `_meta` case with zero regressions). Test tasks below are additive methods in the existing test file; they must be written and shown to FAIL (RED) before the implementation task that turns them GREEN.
+
+**Organization**: Tasks are grouped by user story so each story is independently implementable and testable.
+
+## Format: `[ID] [P?] [Story] Description`
+
+- **[P]**: Can run in parallel (different files, no dependencies on incomplete tasks). Marked sparingly below and justified in the Dependencies section.
+- **[Story]**: US1 / US2 / US3 (maps to the user stories in spec.md). Setup, Foundational, and Polish phases carry **no** story label.
+- Exact file paths are included in every task.
+
+## Path Conventions
+
+Single plugin-script project inside a larger monorepo. Only `plugins/**` changes; the Rust workspace crates are untouched.
+
+- Main adapter: `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`
+- Distributed twin: `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`
+- Test file: `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py`
+- Baseline facts (measured in this checkout at generation time): both `task_handoff.py` copies are 27 443 bytes, SHA-256 `2aeae4523fe92f5cb728146ef2064abbbcb109821dd196070576ff7f68608fc3`, byte-identical; `test_task_handoff.py` reports `Ran 9 tests … OK`. The guard to relax is the `tools/call` branch of `_mcp_stdio()` at ~line 495 (`if set(params) - {"name", "arguments"}:`).
+
+---
+
+## Phase 1: Setup (Shared Infrastructure)
+
+**Purpose**: Pin the pre-change invariants this feature must preserve, so later comparison tasks (FR-007/SC-004 byte identity, FR-009/SC-005 no-regression) compare against measured numbers rather than prose.
+
+- [X] T001 Verify and record the pre-change baseline from this checkout: (a) byte-compare `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` and `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` and record their common SHA-256 (expected to be identical at 27 443 bytes); (b) run `python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_task_handoff` and record the green result (expected `Ran 9 tests … OK`). Write the recorded hash and test count into the PR body as the baseline for FR-007/SC-004 and FR-009/SC-005. No source file is modified by this task. **RESULT (measured on this machine, Python 3.11.9):** both copies byte-identical, common SHA-256 `2aeae4523fe92f5cb728146ef2064abbbcb109821dd196070576ff7f68608fc3`, 27 443 bytes each; suite `Ran 9 tests in ~1.2s … OK`, exit code 0.
+
+**Checkpoint**: Baseline hash and green test count are recorded; the invariant to protect is fixed.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 2: Foundational (Blocking Prerequisites)
+
+**Purpose**: Fix the single edit site that every code task targets, so no story edits the wrong or a duplicated guard point. Completing this phase unblocks US1, US2, and US3.
+
+**⚠️ CRITICAL**: All `task_handoff.py` edits assume this task has confirmed the single inline guard.
+
+- [X] T002 In `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, locate and freeze the sole `tools/call` params-validation site inside `_mcp_stdio()`: the sequence `params = request_value.get("params")` → `if not isinstance(params, dict)` → `if set(params) - {"name", "arguments"}:` → `response = _mcp_result(request_id, _mcp_tool_call(params.get("name"), params.get("arguments"), host, session))` (~lines 491–497). Confirm by search that there is exactly ONE such site (no extracted helper, no second validation point), so per research R2 the fix stays inline with no new surface (G2). Note the membership-vs-`.get` subtlety from data-model §Entity 1 step 5 and research R2: an explicit JSON `null` must be rejected, and `params.get("_meta")` conflates "missing" with "null", so the new type check MUST use an `"_meta" in params` membership test. Record the confirmed line range in the PR body. **RESULT:** confirmed lines 492–497 — `params = request_value.get("params")` (492), object check (493–494), set-diff guard `if set(params) - {"name", "arguments"}:` (495), dispatch `_mcp_tool_call(params.get("name"), params.get("arguments"), host, session)` (497). Grep for `set(params)` returns exactly ONE match (line 495); grep for the dispatch call returns exactly ONE match (line 497). No extracted helper, no second validation point — fix stays inline. The new type check uses `"_meta" in params` (membership), never `.get`.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 3: User Story 1 - Native MCP clients can actually use the task-handoff tools (Priority: P1) 🎯 MVP
+
+**Goal**: Admit an object-valued `_meta` on `tools/call` and dispatch it identically to the same call without `_meta` (FR-001, FR-002): same tool selected, same effective arguments, same result semantics.
+
+**Independent Test**: Start the server on stdio with a valid host/session identity and send one well-formed `tools/call` whose params carry the tool name, tool arguments, and an object `_meta`. Expect a normal tool `result` (not a `-32602` invalid request) — proving the call reached dispatch. This is exactly T003.
+
+> **Within this story, tests come first and must FAIL before the implementation task.**
+
